### Notices — Homebase API
### Base URL: http://localhost:3000
###
### Owners send, tenants receive. Recipients are resolved server-side and
### materialised on the notice at send time, so the audience a request names is
### validated against the sender's own properties and tenants.
###
### Only owners may POST /notices. A tenant calling it gets 403, and the list at
### GET /notices means "what I sent" for an owner and "what I received" for a
### tenant. All audiences resolve to ACTIVE tenancies only, so a terminated
### tenant is never addressed.
###
### Before sending, replace the tokens below with:
###   @ownerToken       the OWNER's bearer token
###   @tenantToken      one of that owner's TENANTS' bearer tokens
###   @otherOwnerToken  a DIFFERENT owner's bearer token (used to prove a notice
###                     cannot be read, marked read, or deleted by a stranger)

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@ownerToken = REPLACE_WITH_OWNER_TOKEN
@tenantToken = REPLACE_WITH_TENANT_TOKEN
@otherOwnerToken = REPLACE_WITH_OTHER_OWNER_TOKEN
@propertyId = REPLACE_WITH_OWNER_PROPERTY_ID
@tenantId = REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_TENANT_ID
@noticeId = REPLACE_WITH_NOTICE_ID

### 1. Send to every tenant across all your properties
### Resolves to each distinct tenant with an active tenancy anywhere you own.
### Neither propertyId nor tenantId may be sent with this audience.
POST {{baseUrl}}/notices
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Water shutoff on Friday",
  "message": "Supply will be off from 9am to 1pm on Friday for tank maintenance. Please store water in advance.",
  "audience": "ALL_PROPERTIES"
}

### 2. Send to every tenant in one property
### The property must belong to you: a foreign id returns 404, not 403, so it
### cannot be probed for existence.
POST {{baseUrl}}/notices
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Gate code change",
  "message": "The main gate code changes to 4417 on Monday. Please update it if you have given it out to anyone.",
  "audience": "PROPERTY",
  "propertyId": "{{propertyId}}"
}

### 3. Send to one tenant
### The target must hold an active tenancy on one of YOUR properties. An
### unrelated tenant returns 404 — this is what stops an owner reaching any
### registered tenant on the platform.
POST {{baseUrl}}/notices
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Your meter reading is due",
  "message": "Please submit your meter reading before the 5th so the invoice is accurate.",
  "audience": "TENANT",
  "tenantId": "{{tenantId}}"
}

### 4. Rejected: PROPERTY without a propertyId (400)
POST {{baseUrl}}/notices
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Missing scope",
  "message": "This cannot be sent because no property was named.",
  "audience": "PROPERTY"
}

### 5. Rejected: a tenant may not send (403)
POST {{baseUrl}}/notices
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Hello neighbours",
  "message": "I am a tenant and this must be rejected.",
  "audience": "ALL_PROPERTIES"
}

### 6. List the notices you sent (owner)
### Each row carries recipientCount and readCount for the per-row summary.
### `propertyId` narrows to notices addressed to that property and excludes
### portfolio-wide notices; `audience` narrows by scope.
GET {{baseUrl}}/notices?page=1&limit=20
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}

### 7. List the notices you sent for one property
GET {{baseUrl}}/notices?propertyId={{propertyId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}

### 8. Your inbox (tenant)
### isRead/readAt are flattened onto each notice from your own receipt. The
### recipient list is withheld — who else a notice went to is not yours to see.
### unreadOnly=true backs the unread filter.
GET {{baseUrl}}/notices?unreadOnly=true
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantToken}}

### 9. Unread count (tenant)
### The single number behind the home bell dot. A non-tenant gets 403.
GET {{baseUrl}}/notices/count
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantToken}}

### 10. Read a notice you received
### Idempotent: re-opening keeps the first readAt, so it reflects when you
### actually first saw it rather than when you last looked.
POST {{baseUrl}}/notices/{{noticeId}}/read
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantToken}}

### 11. Read it as the owner (403) — read state belongs to the recipient
POST {{baseUrl}}/notices/{{noticeId}}/read
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}

### 12. Open a notice as its recipient (tenant)
GET {{baseUrl}}/notices/{{noticeId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantToken}}

### 13. Open it as the owner (full recipient list and read states)
GET {{baseUrl}}/notices/{{noticeId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}

### 14. Open a notice you were not addressed (404, not 403)
### Send notice 2 to one property only, then try to read it from a tenant in a
### different property: the id is reported missing so it cannot be confirmed.
GET {{baseUrl}}/notices/{{noticeId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantToken}}

### 15. Read or delete another owner's notice (404, not 403)
### A 403 here would confirm the id exists and leak the presence of another
### landlord's announcements.
GET {{baseUrl}}/notices/{{noticeId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{otherOwnerToken}}

### 16. Delete a notice you sent (recipient rows cascade away)
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/notices/{{noticeId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{ownerToken}}

### 17. Delete it as a tenant (404) — tenants cannot retract a notice
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/notices/{{noticeId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{tenantToken}}